title: eureka_context
---
// Eureka, the institute town of the Science dimension (gate address
// SCIENCE), on the ground under the orbital station: one node per resident,
// named `eureka_<resident>`, and the institute's own Patent Clerk and Carl
// Stargan. The space elevator goes up from the town's ground station to the
// station's tether hub.
//
// QED-TV, the station on the lecture hall's terrace: Will Force, the
// Science Horse, anchors its evening news; Petra Dish keeps its front desk,
// Avery Gadro produces and Barry Metric does the weather. His news is
// written here for now. Later, a broadcast can report the world's changes
// to the player (`docs/planning/game/reactive-characters-and-dialogue.md`).
//
// Everyday talk only (Jon, 2026-10-29: "we are world building not quest
// building"): no flags, no errands.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: eureka_teodora
---
Teodora: Step onto the car and it goes up. Hold the rail, and do not lean on the door.
-> How far up does it go?
    Teodora: All the way to the station's tether hub. Thirty-six thousand kilometres of cable, and I have walked none of it.
    Teodora: There are two operators on each shift. I work the ground end. Priya asked us to write down both clocks when we close the doors. I have given the other operator a second notebook.
-> What is up there?
    Teodora: Customs, a docking bay, people who eat out of tubes. Further along the spoke, Gnu-ton's gravity works.
    Teodora: The empty car comes back lighter some days. Nobody can tell me why. I log it anyway.
-> How do I get back to Eureka?
    Teodora: SCIENCE is this gate. GALACTIC is the station dock. You can dial either from the other end if you don't fancy the elevator.
    <<command "world.set_flag" "gate.known.SCIENCE" true>>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "gate.known.GALACTIC" true>>
    Teodora: I take the car both ways. I've had quite enough of the recovery benches.
-> Have a good shift.
===

title: eureka_priya
---
Priya: Sorry, I thought you were the next shift. Do you know where Mina is?
-> How long have you been awake?
    Priya: Four days since I had a night off. I slept in the lab last night and woke up when the temperature alarm went off.
-> What are you measuring?
    Priya: Whether a clock at the top of the elevator runs fast. It does. The question is by how much, and whether my supervisor believes me.
    Priya: Teodora is logging both ends for me. I still need to compare the signals properly. I have to account for the delay in the timing signals before I can use those readings.
-> You should rest.
    Priya: Mina keeps telling me. She offered to ring my supervisor for me. I'd rather she didn't.
-> Good luck with it.
===

title: eureka_dmitri
---
Dmitri: Do not touch that. And do not touch that. You can touch that, it is already broken.
-> What do you fix?
    Dmitri: Everything that has a plug. Some things that do not. Last week a professor brought me a chalkboard that was "giving wrong answers".
-> What breaks most?
    Dmitri: The coil lab. Third fuse this week. They say the coils are fine. The fuses disagree.
-> What is the lever by the bench?
    Dmitri: The director's anti-gravity prototype. Last time we ran it, all the pipes came away from the wall.
    Dmitri: If you press it, there is another one on the ceiling. I put it there after the first time.
-> Do you ever go up the elevator?
    Dmitri: Once a month, to fix the car's brakes. Up there I fix the coffee machine too. I take my lunch up with me now.
-> Do you ever work at the mine?
    Dmitri: I used to service the cage. Inez sent a message about the deeper workings. She wants the crusher inspected before the next shift.
-> I will leave you to it.
===

title: eureka_nikobolt
---
Nicholas Tezla: Please keep the blueprints off the floor. I have counted them twice, and I would rather not count them a third time.
<<command "conversation.join" "npc_science_coulomba">>
<<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
Coulomba Tezla: You counted them three times. The third time you counted the napkins too.
-> What are you building?
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: A way to light the whole town without stretching a wire from house to house. The small lamp works at three metres. I can get a good deal more than that out of the large coil.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: The small lamp works because I made him put it in a glass case. Before that, it lit the curtains as well.
-> Is that the gravity lever?
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: A prototype. It changes which way the room thinks is down. Try it if you like, but leave the drawings here.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: Dmitri installed the second switch on the ceiling. We still have a bucket under the sink.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: I told Dmitri I would pay for the pipe.
-> Why do you guard your drawings?
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: A visitor took a copy of my coil drawings. I've since heard about a lightning machine at Castle Godwin.
    Nicholas Tezla: I'd like to know how much of my design they've used. I have no intention of going there to ask.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: We keep copies now. And I have started writing down who comes through the door.
-> Are you two always here together?
    Coulomba Tezla: No. Sometimes he is on the roof counting pigeons, and I am here explaining why they are not my cousins.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: I never said they were.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: You made a family tree.
-> I'll leave you to your work.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: Thank you. Coulomba, where did you put the town plan?
===

title: eureka_coulomba
---
Coulomba Tezla: Come in. The crackling is normal. If you smell smoke, please tell Dmitri before you tell Nicholas.
<<command "conversation.join" "npc_science_nikobolt">>
<<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
Nicholas Tezla: I can smell smoke.
<<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
Coulomba Tezla: Then turn that one off, please.
-> Does he ever leave the laboratory?
    Coulomba Tezla: We walk through the quad in the evenings. We've been trying to get Wilbur to let us replace the lamp by his shed.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: One of those lamps has a loose contact.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: Yes, I know. He says if you dig up his flowers he'll throw the lamp into the duck pond.
-> How did you meet?
    Coulomba Tezla: He was demonstrating a lamp that needed no wire. He'd left out the switch. I asked how we were supposed to turn the thing off.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: She was the only person who asked about the switch.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: He came round the next day with a working switch and a drawing of an even bigger lamp.
-> Are the pigeons outside your family?
    Coulomba Tezla: I am a pigeon. That does not make every pigeon in town my relation.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: We have no proof either way.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: We also have no reason to collect feathers for your proposed test.
-> Can I touch the coils?
    Coulomba Tezla: Wait until I've turned the power off.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_nikobolt">>
    Nicholas Tezla: The coils are insulated.
    <<present_speaker "npc_science_coulomba">>
    Coulomba Tezla: Since Tuesday.
-> Good to meet you both.
    Coulomba Tezla: Come by again. Perhaps we will have finished the lamp for the street.
===

title: eureka_wilbur
---
Wilbur: Mind the grass. I keep it. It does not keep itself, whatever the physicists tell you.
-> Have you worked here long?
    Wilbur: Forty years on this quad. Three deans, two fires, one theory of everything. The second dean was the one who let them put a cable under my lawn.
-> Do the other pigeons bother you?
    Wilbur: Do not start. They are not my relatives. They are not anyone's relatives. They eat crumbs off the lecture steps and look at me like I owe them.
-> What do you think of the elevator?
    Wilbur: They promised me a little cart when they built it. I've asked every year. Teodora offered to bring a trolley down from the station. I told her it needs wheels that work on grass.
-> I will mind the grass.
===

title: eureka_halvorsen
---
Professor Halvorsen: You there. You look impartial. Read this paragraph and tell me it is clear.
-> What is it?
    Professor Halvorsen: My paper. Reviewer two says it is "unclear in places". He does not say which places. He never says which places.
-> Why are you in Eureka?
    Professor Halvorsen: I came through the gate to give one lecture. That was three months ago. I have been revising ever since.
    Professor Halvorsen: Math Town wanted me first. They said physics was "applied". I said they were "unemployed". We have not spoken.
-> Is reviewer two right?
    Professor Halvorsen: That is precisely the kind of question reviewer two would ask.
-> Good luck with the revisions.
===

title: eureka_mina
---
Mina: Coffee, soup, or a chair. The chair is free if you buy something.
-> Who comes here?
    Mina: Everybody on campus. Students in the morning, professors at noon, the lab crew when something has caught fire.
    Mina: Physics owes me eleven coffees. Chemistry pays in cash. Biology brings its own cups, which I respect.
-> What is the soup?
    Mina: Whatever came down on the elevator. Today it is lentils from the station. Pell sent an apology about the spinach. They needed the cold box for a lab shipment.
    Mina: I sent the empty tins back with a note asking for more lentils. Everyone liked them.
-> Do people from the station come down?
    Mina: On weekends. They ask for the table by the window. I keep it free until the elevator comes down.
-> Just looking.
===

title: eureka_patent_clerk
---
Patent Clerk: Application received. Please take a number. Numbers are issued in the order they are observed.
-> What are you stamping?
    Patent Clerk: Claims. A perpetual motion machine, a faster elevator, and a hat that thinks. Two rejections and one request for a better drawing.
-> Is this where you work now?
    Patent Clerk: An office is an office. This one has a window on the cable. When I watch the car go up, my clock and the operator's disagree. I file that too.
-> What do you think of the elevator?
    Patent Clerk: It is a fine machine with a terrible timetable. Car up at the hour. Whose hour? Measured from where?
    Patent Clerk: Teodora says "the hour on the wall". I asked which wall. She has stopped answering my letters.
-> Carry on.
===

title: eureka_stargan
---
<<portrait_clip "wonder">>
Carl Stargan: Look up. No, further. That pale dot just above the cable is the station. Everyone you have met up there lives on that dot.
-> What do you study here?
    <<portrait_clip "explaining">>
    Carl Stargan: The sky. Billions and billions of stars, and on a clear night the elevator's cable cuts right through the middle of them.
-> Is the science dimension the only one with stars?
    <<portrait_clip "skeptical">>
    Carl Stargan: Math Town insists they are a separate dimension and their sky is "a set". I asked them to measure it. They proved it exists and went home.
-> You file reports for QED-TV?
    <<portrait_clip "explaining">>
    Carl Stargan: I am its astronomy correspondent. Will gives me ninety seconds. I use them to say "look up", and then the sky takes the rest.
    <<portrait_clip "skeptical">>
    Carl Stargan: He has never once cut me off. He has, twice, combed his hair during a supernova.
-> Can I use the telescope?
    <<portrait_clip "explaining">>
    Carl Stargan: Of course. Look at the station first, then past it. It is good for you to see how small a big thing is.
-> Thank you.
===

title: eureka_pryce
---
Range Officer Pryce: Hard hat? No? Then stand behind me. Not that I am any harder.
-> What is tested here?
    Range Officer Pryce: Rockets, coils, a self-driving wheelbarrow. Whatever the institute builds, it comes out here to be found out.
    Range Officer Pryce: I sign the form. The form has two boxes: FINE and PROBABLY FINE. Guess which I tick.
-> Is the rocket safe?
    Range Officer Pryce: PROBABLY FINE III? It has a name with three in it. The first two taught us a great deal, briefly.
-> What are those dummies for?
    Range Officer Pryce: Crash tests. Hit one. Go on. They are paid in sandbags and they do not complain, which is more than I can say for the interns.
-> Do you ever get a quiet day?
    Range Officer Pryce: Thursdays. Everybody has to write up the results of Wednesday. I bring a book.
-> Stay safe.
    Range Officer Pryce: Probably.
===

title: eureka_hum
---
Ottoline Hum: (She holds one hand near the antenna, the other by the loop. A note wavers out of nothing, like a ghost clearing its throat.)
-> How do you play it without touching it?
    Ottoline Hum: Your hand and the antenna make a capacitor. Move closer, the pitch goes up. The other hand on the loop is the volume. You play the air between.
    Ottoline Hum: The trick is standing still. Breathe wrong and you're a semitone flat.
-> What are you playing?
    Ottoline Hum: Whatever the institute's working on. Monday it was the hydrogen spectrum. The physicists cried. The chemists asked for it in a minor key.
-> Do people pay?
    Ottoline Hum: Visitors off the gate pay in coins I've never seen. I've got a case of forty different currencies. The patent clerk says one of them is technically a button.
-> Do you play anywhere else?
    Ottoline Hum: Occasionally in the cafe. Mina put a table too close to me once. Every time somebody reached for the sugar I played a little trill.
-> Play on.
    Ottoline Hum: Always. Mind you don't walk between me and the antenna. You'd be in the song.
===

title: eureka_quark
---
Dr. Ilse Quark: Visitor! Helmet's optional, curiosity isn't. Welcome to the Eureka Collider. This is the slow bit. The fast bit is in the pipe.
-> What does the collider do?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: We send two beams round the ring opposite ways and make them meet in the detector. Then we read the wreckage. It's the most expensive way anyone has ever found to break something to see what's in it.
-> Why the bicycles?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: The ring's twenty-seven kilometres round. The beam does it eleven thousand times a second. I do it once before lunch. We don't race. Well. I don't.
-> What has got loose in the tunnel?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: The range's crawlers come down the cable ducts for the warmth, and a shock warden followed them. Nobody's sure which way the shock warden's spin points. Mind it.
-> What is the quark in the chest?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: A spare. Charm flavour. You can't have a quark on its own, strictly, so don't open the box in front of a theorist.
===

title: eureka_will_force
---
Will Force: Good evening, Eureka. I'm Will Force, and this is the news, straight from the horse's mouth.
Will Force: You are not a camera. That's fine. I never waste a good evening.
-> What is in the news tonight?
    Will Force: Tonight's top stories. One: Priya at the institute says a clock at the top of the tether runs fast.
    Will Force: That is not a broken clock. That is gravity. Time goes a little faster the higher you are out of a gravity well. A little. Not enough to be late for my show.
    Will Force: Two: Teodora reports that the tether's car comes back lighter on some days. A developing story. QED-TV does not report a mystery as a fact. We report it as a mystery, with a serious face.
    Will Force: And beyond the gate, lightning over a castle in another dimension. Sources say someone borrowed a coil design. Our source is Coulomba.
    Will Force: I'm Will Force. Stay curious, Eureka.
-> Show me a demonstration.
    Will Force: Live, on my set? Absolutely. Stand behind the tape.
    Will Force: This is a Van de Graaff generator. A rubber belt carries charge up into the sphere, and the charge spreads over its outside.
    Will Force: Touch the sphere and the charge spreads over you too. Each hair gets the same charge, and like charges push each other away. So each hair stands up and tries to leave the others.
    Will Force: And that is why I do not touch the sphere. The science is free. The hair is insured.
-> Why no safety goggles?
    Will Force: Goggles leave a line in the fur, and the fur is on camera. I stand outside the tape. You must wear goggles past the tape. Do as I say, not as I wear.
-> What is one horsepower?
    Will Force: Seven hundred and forty-six watts. A man who sold steam engines measured horses turning mills, so his buyers could compare. My people became a unit of sales.
    Will Force: Here is the twist: a real horse can make nearly fifteen horsepower for a moment, and less than one over a working day. I make one whole broadcast, every night. Television is endurance.
-> Do you work with Carl Stargan?
    Will Force: Carl is our astronomy correspondent. He files from the observatory. Every report begins with "look up", and every report runs long. I allow it. The universe is a big story.
    Will Force: Neil onGras Turfson files from the orbital station. Well, actually, he corrects my reports from the orbital station. Live.
    Will Force: Great colleagues. Wonderful men. Neither of them has the hair.
-> Who are you?
    Will Force: (He looks at his reflection in the dark lens of a camera, then at you.) You don't know?
    Will Force: Will Force, the Science Horse. Anchor of the QED-TV evening news. Three EMMC-squared awards. People recognise me in four dimensions.
    Will Force: I explain the universe at six o'clock, and I explain it correctly. I check every fact twice. The hair I check eleven times.
-> Goodnight.
    Will Force: Goodnight, and stay curious, Eureka. (He waits.) Was that on air? Avery! Was that on air?
===

title: eureka_petra_dish
---
Petra Dish: Welcome to QED-TV. Sign in, please. If you are here for the horse, take a seat. Everyone is here for the horse.
-> What is in the dish on your desk?
    Petra Dish: A culture. Yeast, mostly, and a little ambition. I feed it sugar on Mondays. It is the only thing in this building that grows without a camera on it.
-> What is an EMMC-squared?
    Petra Dish: The institute's award for broadcasting. E for excellence, M for media, and then Mr. Force said C squared, because energy. Nobody stopped him. He has three.
    Petra Dish: Two of them are the same award. He asked for a second one for the other side of the desk. He said it was for balance.
-> Who comes to the studio?
    Petra Dish: Professors who want to explain a paper in ninety seconds. Students who want to be in the audience. Halvorsen, every day, in case reviewer two is watching.
    Petra Dish: Carl Stargan comes in from the observatory, and Neil onGras Turfson calls from the station. I give them both coffee and then I give Mr. Force a mirror.
-> I'll take a seat.
    Petra Dish: The orange couch. It was in the first broadcast. Please do not jump on it. People jump on it.
===

title: eureka_avery_gadro
---
Avery Gadro: Ninety seconds to air. Do not touch the board. Do not stand on the board. Why is everyone always on the board?
-> What does a producer do?
    Avery Gadro: Everything that the anchor does not see. The cameras, the lights, the guests, the cues, the prompter. On a good night I count six times ten to the twenty-third little problems. That is about one mole of problems.
-> What happened on air last week?
    Avery Gadro: Someone typed a question mark on the teleprompter. He read it exactly as it was. "Good evening. I'm Will Force?"
    Avery Gadro: Then he said, "That is a good question," and he talked about identity for eleven minutes. The ratings went up. I hate that the ratings went up.
-> Is he careful with the science?
    Avery Gadro: Completely. He sends every story to two professors and to Dmitri, because Dmitri checks whether the thing has a plug. If a fact is wrong, he will not go on air.
    Avery Gadro: If his hair is wrong, he also will not go on air. One of those is a policy I wrote.
-> Good luck with the show.
    Avery Gadro: Luck is not a variable I budget for. Thank you anyway.
===

title: eureka_barry_metric
---
Barry Metric: Good evening. Pressure is falling. Not my pressure. The air's.
-> Why is the wall green?
    Barry Metric: The camera replaces everything green with the weather map. So on television I stand in front of the sky, and here I stand in front of a wall.
    Barry Metric: I must never wear green. One night I wore a green tie and the viewers saw a hole in me with a cloud in it.
-> What does a barometer measure?
    Barry Metric: The weight of all the air over you, pressing down. When it falls, the air is rising, and rising air makes clouds. Bring a coat.
-> What is the weather at the top of the tether?
    Barry Metric: None. There is no air up there to have weather in. It is the easiest forecast I give. Will still makes me give it.
-> Thanks, Barry.
    Barry Metric: Tomorrow: sunny, then less sunny. I am almost always right about the second half.
===
